        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "81e5670f-2a23-5d6b-bf26-38d36ab74abe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Hart Crane, John le Carré, T. S. Eliot, David John Moore Cornwell\n- Works: The Bridge, The Waste Land, The Broken Tower\n- Organizations: Security Service, Secret Intelligence Service\n- Topics: Epic poetry, modern urban culture, espionage novels, pen names\n\nFacts:\n- In 1932, Hart Crane’s poem \"The Broken Tower\" was the last new poem intended for publication during his lifetime.\n- \"The Broken Tower\" has been widely regarded as one of the best lyrics of Hart Crane’s final years, and possibly of his career.\n- In his most ambitious work, \"The Bridge,\" Hart Crane sought to write an epic poem in the vein of T. S. Eliot’s \"The Waste Land.\"\n- In \"The Bridge,\" Hart Crane aimed to express a more optimistic view of modern urban culture than the view he found in T. S. Eliot’s work.\n- David John Moore Cornwell was born on 19 October 1931.\n- David John Moore Cornwell was known by the pseudonym John le Carré.\n- John le Carré was a British author of espionage novels.\n- During the 1950s and 1960s, John le Carré worked for the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service.\n- During the 1950s and 1960s, John le Carré began writing novels under his pen name.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "eb23ff6c-25ef-5a6c-baa2-d2f7a5ba7f8a",
        "text": "Page: Hart Crane. Sentence: In his most ambitious work, \"The Bridge\", Crane sought to write an epic poem, in the vein of \"The Waste Land\", that expressed a more optimistic view of modern, urban culture than the one that he found in Eliot's work.\nPage: John le Carré. Sentence: David John Moore Cornwell (born 19 October 1931), known by the pseudonym John le Carré ( ), is a British author of espionage novels.\nPage: John le Carré. Sentence: During the 1950s and the 1960s, he worked for both the Security Service and the Secret Intelligence Service, and began writing novels under his pen name.\nPage: The Broken Tower. Sentence: The last new poem meant to be published in Hart Crane's life, \"The Broken Tower\" (1932) has been widely acknowledged as one of the best lyrics of Crane's last years, if not his career.",
